berkeley-principles-3.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered yes, which keeps berkeley-principles-3.logic.r2 and berkeley-principles-3.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "by the word spirit we mean only that which thinks, wills and perceives" is true and "necessarily, by the word spirit we mean only that which thinks, wills and perceives" is false. Is S3, "For, by the word spirit we mean only that which thinks, wills, and perceives; this, and this alone, constitutes the signification of the term.", true then?
berkeley-principles-3.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: berkeley-principles-3.logic.r2 has "spirit_only → ¬◇like_other" and "◇rep_other → ◇like_other" and "◇idea_spirit → ◇rep_powers ∨ ◇rep_other"; berkeley-principles-3.logic.r2.2 has "□(spirit_only → ¬like_other)" and "□(rep_other → like_other)" and "□(idea_spirit → rep_powers ∨ rep_other)"; they differ when only "necessarily, (if an idea represents a spirit in some other thing than those powers, then there remains something else, besides willing, thinking and perceiving, wherein the idea is like a spirit)", "necessarily, (if there is an idea of a spirit, then (either some degree of the powers of thinking, willing and perceiving is represented in an idea or an idea represents a spirit in some other thing than those powers))", "possibly, there remains something else, besides willing, thinking and perceiving, wherein the idea is like a spirit", "possibly, there remains something else, besides willing, thinking and perceiving, wherein the idea is like a spirit", "possibly, an idea represents a spirit in some other thing than those powers" and "possibly, an idea represents a spirit in some other thing than those powers" are true, which the first allows; berkeley-principles-3.logic.r2.3 has "spirit_only → ¬◇like_other" and "□(rep_other → like_other)" and "□(idea_spirit → rep_powers ∨ rep_other)"
berkeley-principles-3.logic.r2: argue/readings: U1: berkeley-principles-3.logic.r2.2 and berkeley-principles-3.logic.r2.3 take for granted "necessarily, (if there is an idea of a spirit, then (either some degree of the powers of thinking, willing and perceiving is represented in an idea or an idea represents a spirit in some other thing than those powers))"; berkeley-principles-3.logic.r2 does not
berkeley-principles-3.logic.r2: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps berkeley-principles-3.logic.r2.2 and berkeley-principles-3.logic.r2.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "necessarily, (if there is an idea of a spirit, then (either some degree of the powers of thinking, willing and perceiving is represented in an idea or an idea represents a spirit in some other thing than those powers))"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
